{
  "requirement": "Under {cite}`requirements.cli.skill_structure`, the `habeas-refactor` skill MUST be the home of moving code, tests, and requirements to another file, chapter, or outline node, and of renaming requirements.",
  "determination": true,
  "rationale": "**Obligation.** `requirements.cli.skill_structure.refactor` has two parts. The `habeas-refactor` skill must state in full how to move code, tests and requirements to another file, chapter or outline node, and how to rename requirements. Under `requirements.cli.skill_structure`, that topic must live in exactly one skill, its home.\n\n**Test.** `tests/authoring_cli.rs#fn[the_refactor_skill_is_home_of_moves_and_requirement_renames]` is marked `{test}` for the subject. It makes three groups of assertions:\n\n1. **Workflow content.** It reads the `## Workflow` section of `habeas-refactor`'s `SKILL.md` and asserts these phrases:\n   - Moving a `{req}` directive to its new chapter.\n   - A file or directory mapping, `--map src/config.rs=src/settings.rs`.\n   - A node mapping, `--map ...`.\n   - A definition mapping, `--map requirements.cli.check=requirements.core.check`.\n\n   Together these cover the file, chapter and node destinations. A moved test is a file or node, so the same mappings cover it.\n2. **Renames.** It asserts that the skill contains \"Renaming a requirement's ID is a definition mapping\", which covers renaming requirements.\n3. **Single home.** `assert_home(\"habeas-refactor\")` calls `assert_phrases` with `home_markers`: `habeas refactor --dry-run`, the node-mapping example, and the \"Would leave ... unchanged\" report text. It requires every marker in `habeas-refactor`. It then loops over the other four `SKILLS` and asserts that none contains any marker. That is the \"exactly one home\" check for this topic.\n\n**Checks against the skill text.** I read `assets/skills/habeas-refactor/SKILL.md`, and it contains every asserted phrase. I grepped the other skills for `--map`, `habeas refactor` and \"rename\". `habeas-evidence` and `habeas-requirements` only point to `habeas-refactor`: a one-line table entry that says to use `habeas refactor --map <old>=<new>` (habeas-refactor), and \"Never rename or move a committed requirement\". None restates the procedure, so the exclusivity assertion is not vacuous and agrees with the sources. The referral direction is covered separately by `requirements.cli.skill_structure.refer`, whose test is `bundled_skills_refer_to_a_topic_home_instead_of_restating_it`.\n\n**Limitation.** Exclusivity is checked only through the three markers, not through every phrase asserted in the workflow. That is the project's uniform approach to home checks across all skills, and the markers do cover the command, the node mapping and the report semantics. I do not count it as a gap. Both the presence and the absence assertions would fail if the topic were moved to or duplicated in another skill.\n\nNo `{fixme}` acknowledgments apply. I found no unresolved gap.",
  "references": "a54d07ae817e846f35af61d3ee5e062c81c43bc70e6a4a504a69071debfe727e",
